# Feature: Fix Codex implementer crash from write_stdin on exec_command session (issue #2991)

## The bug
Codex implementer (`scripts/launch-codex-implement.sh` → `codex exec --full-auto ...`) completed all implementation work and `make test-harnesses-13` (all 36 assertions passed), then crashed before writing manifest.json:
  ERROR codex_core::tools::router: write_stdin failed: stdin is closed for this session; rerun exec_command with tty=true to keep stdin open
Result: 674 uncommitted lines in working tree, no manifest, dispatch timed out (exit 4).

## Root cause
Codex CLI exposes interactive-subprocess tools (`exec_command`, `write_stdin`, `read_stdout`). Codex spawned a child via `exec_command` (without tty=true) then tried `write_stdin` against it. That tool call requires a TTY-allocated session and fails when stdin is closed. The failure was not in larch's parent shell — it was Codex internally trying to use an interactive subprocess pattern.

## Related, already-in-flight issue
#2973 (blocking this) fixes a DIFFERENT failure mode: parent shell closes Codex's own stdin, triggering `codex_core::session: failed to record rollout items` errors in voter and Step 5 paths. The #2973 fix adds `< /dev/null` to all background Codex spawns in `scripts/run-external-agent.sh:206-213`. That fix lands BEFORE this one and does NOT prevent Codex from trying to use interactive-subprocess tools against its own children.

## What this design must do (decisions from Round 1)
1. Pursue Option 2 ONLY (prompt-level prohibition). No `step2-implement.sh` recovery branch. No setsid/PTY wrapper.
2. Apply the fix to BOTH the Codex Step 2 implementer prompt AND the Codex CI fixer prompt. The Codex implementer prompt source is `agents/_implementer-base.md` (canonical, AUTO-GENERATED to `agents/codex-implementer.md` and `agents/cursor-implementer.md` via `scripts/generate-codex-implementer.sh` and `scripts/generate-cursor-implementer.sh`). The Codex CI fixer prompt is constructed inline in `scripts/launch-codex-ci.sh` starting at line 138.
3. The prohibition is broader than just `write_stdin`: it must prohibit any pattern that creates persistent interactive subprocess sessions (`exec_command` with kept-alive sessions, paired `read_stdout` polling). The prompt should give positive guidance: when input is needed, use heredocs, pipes, or input files — never an interactive session.
4. Cursor-implementer.md does not need this guard (Cursor agent CLI does not expose those tools), but if the change lands in the shared base file with a parenthetical "(Codex-specific)" annotation, that is acceptable.

## Repo conventions to preserve
- `.claude/rules/script-md-siblings.md`: every `.sh` has a sibling `.md`. Edits to `scripts/launch-codex-ci.sh` must update `scripts/launch-codex-ci.md`.
- `.claude/rules/external-tool-launcher-parity.md`: Codex/Cursor launcher edits should be considered together — but for this issue the prohibition is Codex-tool-name-specific, so parity does not require the same guard in Cursor places.
- `.claude/rules/launcher-argv-test-coverage.md`: if any launcher argv is touched, the matching harness must be updated in the same PR. (This design likely touches prompt CONSTRUCTION, not argv, so this rule may or may not apply.)
- `agents/codex-implementer.md` is AUTO-GENERATED — do NOT hand-edit; edit `agents/_implementer-base.md` and regenerate via `bash scripts/generate-codex-implementer.sh`.
- `make lint` must pass after changes.

